1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
A prep room shelf holds eight bottles of

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,640
solutions, and the labels have washed off.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,480
Each one holds a different metal ion: barium,

4
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:13,120
calcium, magnesium, lead, silver, copper, and iron in

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,400
two forms, iron two and iron three.

6
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,640
A sample from one bottle is in this

7
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:19,520
tube.

8
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,080
A blue tint might hint at copper, but

9
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,920
a hint isn't proof.

10
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:26,560
One test won't settle eight suspects.

11
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,640
We need a sequence of tests, where each

12
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,800
result rules some ions out.

13
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,760
We've got three kinds of evidence.

14
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,680
A precipitate test adds an anion that makes

15
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:41,360
an insoluble solid with only some cations, using

16
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:44,000
the solubility rules from Module 5.

17
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:46,480
A flame test heats the sample and reads

18
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,080
the colour of the flame.

19
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,280
And a complexation test adds molecules or ions

20
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:53,520
that bond around the metal ion.

21
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,760
That makes a complex ion, which often has

22
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,400
a striking colour, or can pull a solid

23
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,080
back into solution.

24
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:03,680
The plan: use precipitates to sort, then flames

25
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:05,600
and complexes to confirm.

26
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:09,840
Start with a fresh portion of the sample,

27
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:12,080
and add dilute hydrochloric acid.

28
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,680
That brings in chloride ions.

29
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,480
Chlorides dissolve, except with silver and lead, so

30
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,760
a white precipitate here means silver or lead,

31
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,280
and nothing else on our list.

32
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,480
No precipitate usually rules both of them out.

33
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:31,040
One catch: lead chloride is only slightly soluble,

34
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:33,520
so a dilute lead sample can slip through

35
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:34,480
to the next step.

36
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:38,980
Both solids are white, so how do you

37
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:40,100
tell them apart?

38
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:42,620
Add dilute ammonia to the solid.

39
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:45,700
Ammonia molecules bond to silver ions and form

40
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:49,020
a complex, so silver chloride dissolves.

41
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:51,100
Lead chloride stays put.

42
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:53,860
For a second check on lead, add potassium

43
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:55,820
iodide to a fresh portion.

44
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,540
Lead gives a bright yellow precipitate, lead iodide.

45
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:04,366
If step one gave nothing, take another fresh

46
00:02:04,447 --> 00:02:07,126
portion and add dilute sulfuric acid.

47
00:02:07,407 --> 00:02:10,686
Barium sulfate is insoluble, so barium gives a

48
00:02:10,767 --> 00:02:11,726
white precipitate.

49
00:02:12,127 --> 00:02:15,566
Calcium sulfate is only slightly soluble, so calcium

50
00:02:15,647 --> 00:02:17,886
shows up here mainly when the sample's fairly

51
00:02:17,967 --> 00:02:18,766
concentrated.

52
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:21,646
And lead sulfate is white too, so any

53
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:23,806
lead that slipped past step one gets caught

54
00:02:23,887 --> 00:02:24,406
here.

55
00:02:24,607 --> 00:02:26,846
A white solid in this step still needs

56
00:02:26,927 --> 00:02:29,006
one more test to say which metal.

57
00:02:31,034 --> 00:02:33,593
That's where the flame test earns its place.

58
00:02:33,914 --> 00:02:36,073
Dip a clean wire loop in the sample,

59
00:02:36,154 --> 00:02:38,473
and hold it in a blue Bunsen flame.

60
00:02:38,794 --> 00:02:41,833
Heat excites electrons in the sample's atoms, and

61
00:02:41,914 --> 00:02:44,073
as they fall back, they give out light

62
00:02:44,234 --> 00:02:45,833
characteristic of the element.

63
00:02:46,074 --> 00:02:47,353
You met this in Year 11.

64
00:02:47,994 --> 00:02:50,233
Barium gives a pale green flame.

65
00:02:50,634 --> 00:02:53,273
Calcium gives brick red, an orange-red.

66
00:02:53,834 --> 00:02:55,433
Copper gives blue-green.

67
00:02:55,914 --> 00:02:58,713
Magnesium and lead give no useful colour, so

68
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:01,273
for lead, go back to the iodide test.

69
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,340
Treat a flame colour as supporting evidence, though,

70
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:07,420
not proof.

71
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:10,660
Traces of sodium are very common, and sodium's

72
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:13,540
bright yellow can swamp a weaker colour.

73
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:15,620
A dirty loop carries colour over from the

74
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:16,900
last sample.

75
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:18,820
So clean the loop in acid until the

76
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:21,460
flame shows no colour, and back up each

77
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,380
flame with a precipitate result.

78
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:28,600
Now the third reagent, sodium hydroxide, in a

79
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:29,480
fresh portion.

80
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:33,320
Most metal hydroxides are insoluble, and four of

81
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,480
our ions give solids you can tell apart

82
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:36,200
by colour.

83
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,560
Copper gives pale blue copper hydroxide.

84
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:43,440
Iron three gives red-brown iron three hydroxide, the

85
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:44,440
colour of rust.

86
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,080
Iron two gives a green solid.

87
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,960
And magnesium gives a white one, which doesn't

88
00:03:50,013 --> 00:03:52,360
dissolve when you add more sodium hydroxide.

89
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,480
A faint white solid could also be calcium

90
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,680
from a concentrated sample, so check the flame:

91
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:00,680
no colour points to magnesium.

92
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:05,500
That's also why the chloride and sulfate steps

93
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:06,860
come first.

94
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:09,500
Silver and lead would form solids with hydroxide

95
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:11,820
too, and muddy the colours.

96
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:13,980
Catching them early means a colour in this

97
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,300
step can only come from the ions still

98
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:16,860
left.

99
00:04:18,867 --> 00:04:21,186
Watch the iron two result closely.

100
00:04:21,667 --> 00:04:24,386
The green solid sits in air, and oxygen

101
00:04:24,547 --> 00:04:27,586
slowly takes electrons from iron two, turning it

102
00:04:27,667 --> 00:04:28,706
into iron three.

103
00:04:29,347 --> 00:04:31,906
So over a few minutes, the green darkens

104
00:04:31,947 --> 00:04:32,826
to brown.

105
00:04:33,107 --> 00:04:35,506
If you read it late, iron two looks

106
00:04:35,627 --> 00:04:36,546
like iron three.

107
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:38,706
Read the colour straight away.

108
00:04:40,734 --> 00:04:42,773
Then confirm with a complex.

109
00:04:43,054 --> 00:04:45,693
Add excess ammonia to the copper result, and

110
00:04:45,734 --> 00:04:48,333
the pale blue solid dissolves into a deep

111
00:04:48,454 --> 00:04:51,213
blue solution, the copper ammonia complex.

112
00:04:51,694 --> 00:04:54,293
Add thiocyanate ions to a fresh portion of

113
00:04:54,334 --> 00:04:57,693
iron three, and the solution turns blood red,

114
00:04:57,734 --> 00:05:00,893
the iron thiocyanate complex from Module 5.

115
00:05:01,134 --> 00:05:03,933
When two different kinds of chemistry agree, the

116
00:05:04,374 --> 00:05:07,373
identification is much stronger than one colour alone.

117
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,640
Put it together and you get a flowchart.

118
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:15,800
Chloride, then sulfate, then hydroxide, each in a

119
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:16,760
fresh portion.

120
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:20,280
Each no rules ions out, so each yes

121
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:21,880
points to fewer ions.

122
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:25,160
Our prep room sample gave nothing with chloride

123
00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:29,000
or sulfate, a pale blue solid with hydroxide,

124
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:31,640
a deep blue complex with ammonia, and a

125
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:33,000
blue-green flame.

126
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:34,120
That's copper.

127
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:37,640
Mixtures need a separation plan, and tests on

128
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:39,080
each separated part.

129
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:42,600
This chart identifies one unknown cation.

130
00:05:44,634 --> 00:05:46,433
Here's a way to hold the order.

131
00:05:46,714 --> 00:05:50,593
Chloride, sulfate, hydroxide, then flame.

132
00:05:50,874 --> 00:05:53,673
Two whites with chloride: silver and lead.

133
00:05:54,154 --> 00:05:56,793
Barium and calcium with sulfate, and the flame

134
00:05:56,874 --> 00:05:57,513
splits them.

135
00:05:57,994 --> 00:06:02,473
Then blue, brown, green, white with hydroxide: copper,

136
00:06:02,554 --> 00:06:05,193
iron three, iron two, magnesium.

137
00:06:05,594 --> 00:06:07,913
And a green that turns brown is iron

138
00:06:07,994 --> 00:06:09,353
two, read late.

139
00:06:11,367 --> 00:06:12,006
Your turn.

140
00:06:12,647 --> 00:06:15,686
Separate portions of an unknown solution give these

141
00:06:16,007 --> 00:06:16,606
results.

142
00:06:16,887 --> 00:06:19,926
Identify the cation, and write the net ionic

143
00:06:20,087 --> 00:06:21,886
equation for the precipitate.

144
00:06:21,927 --> 00:06:23,286
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:37,940
No chloride precipitate rules out silver, but dilute

146
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:39,300
lead can slip through.

147
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:42,820
The white sulfate and pale green flame identify

148
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:43,460
barium.

149
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:47,700
So barium plus sulfate gives solid barium sulfate.

150
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:50,420
Calling it calcium just because it's a white

151
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,580
sulfate is the classic slip.

152
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:55,240
Last one.

153
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,680
A student adds sodium hydroxide to a pale

154
00:06:58,760 --> 00:06:59,800
green solution.

155
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:03,240
A green precipitate forms, but ten minutes later

156
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:07,080
it's red-brown, and the student concludes iron three.

157
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:11,080
Evaluate this conclusion, and suggest a confirming test.

158
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:13,000
Pause here to answer.

159
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:26,040
The first colour counts.

160
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:29,480
A green precipitate is iron two hydroxide, and

161
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:32,040
it turned brown because oxygen in the air

162
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:34,360
oxidised it to iron three.

163
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:36,360
So the cation is iron two, and the

164
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:38,040
conclusion's wrong.

165
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:41,720
To confirm, add thiocyanate to a fresh portion:

166
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,520
no blood red rules out iron three.

167
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:46,920
Trusting the final colour is the trap.

168
00:07:48,934 --> 00:07:51,733
So the cation comes from a short sequence:

169
00:07:51,854 --> 00:07:54,533
precipitates to sort, then a flame or a

170
00:07:54,614 --> 00:07:55,973
complex to confirm.

171
00:07:56,614 --> 00:07:59,013
Next, the other half of the salt: how

172
00:07:59,094 --> 00:08:00,853
do we identify the anion?

173
00:08:02,867 --> 00:08:04,306
Here are the key notes.

174
00:08:04,547 --> 00:08:06,466
Pause the video if you need more time

175
00:08:06,547 --> 00:08:07,346
to copy them.
